          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "glog-bcc778de-39af-47cb-a231-319c4da2bcc8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string up to but not including the first null character. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet `strlen((*kv)`, it seems like `kv` is a pointer to a string. If the string pointed to by `kv` is not null-terminated, this can lead to the aforementioned issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character at the end, or by manually adding a null character at the end of your strings before using `strlen`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `kv` is a pointer to a string, you should ensure that the string is null-terminated before using `strlen`. Here is an example of how you can do this:\n\n```cpp\nchar* kv = new char[100];\n// ... (code to populate kv)\nkv[99] = '\\0'; // Ensure kv is null-terminated\nsize_t len = strlen(kv);\n```\n\nIn this example, `kv` is a pointer to a string of 100 characters. Before using `strlen`, we manually add a null character at the end of the string to ensure that it is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` function is part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-475ff52f-1948-492b-a5a6-decb646c3c05",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string up to but not including the first null character. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet `strlen((*kv)`, it seems like `kv` is a pointer to a string. If the string pointed to by `kv` is not null-terminated, this can lead to the aforementioned issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character at the end, or by manually adding a null character at the end of your strings before using `strlen`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `kv` is a pointer to a string, you should ensure that the string is null-terminated before using `strlen`. Here is an example of how you can do this:\n\n```cpp\nchar* kv = new char[100];\n// ... (code to populate kv)\nkv[99] = '\\0'; // Ensure kv is null-terminated\nsize_t len = strlen(kv);\n```\n\nIn this example, `kv` is a pointer to a string of 100 characters. Before using `strlen`, we manually add a null character at the end of the string to ensure that it is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` function is part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
