            {
              "id": "glog-df32c004-f5fc-4670-b469-97885eeab204",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee null-termination of the destination string when the source string length is greater than or equal to the destination size. This can lead to buffer overflows, which are a common source of security vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution or denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, consider using safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination, such as `strlcpy` or `strncpy_s`. If these are not available, you can manually null-terminate the string after copying.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(cpu_info->model_name, model_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\ncpu_info->model_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis ensures that `model_name` is always null-terminated, preventing potential buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: for the `strncpy` function\n- `<string>`: for the `std::string` class\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the availability of these links is subject to change and they may not be accessible for all users."
