                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee that the destination string will be null-terminated, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. In the provided code snippet, the `strncpy` function is used to copy a string from `driver_name.c_str()` to `info->driver_name` with a maximum length of `AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1`. If the source string is longer than the destination buffer, the destination string will not be null-terminated, which can lead to undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination of the destination string. In C++, the `std::string` class provides safe string handling functionality. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with the `std::string::copy` function, which ensures null-termination of the destination string:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string driver_name_str = driver_name.c_str();\ndriver_name_str.copy(info->driver_name, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1);\ninfo->driver_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` class and its methods.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
