            {
              "id": "glog-ff839176-dc96-4280-820e-280275e0d164",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee null-termination of the destination string when the source string length is greater than or equal to the size of the destination buffer. This can lead to buffer overflows, which are a common source of security vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution or denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, consider using safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination, such as `strlcpy` or `strncpy_s`. If these are not available, you can manually null-terminate the string after copying, but be sure to account for the size of the destination buffer to avoid overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's a possible fix using `strncpy_s`:\n\n```cpp\nerrno_t err = strncpy_s(info->part_number, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn), _TRUNCATE);\nif (err) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\nOr, if `strncpy_s` is not available, manually null-terminate the string:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(info->part_number, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\ninfo->part_number[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `cstring` for `strncpy`\n- `cerrno` for `errno_t` and error handling with `strncpy_s`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
