            {
              "id": "glog-90cac68d-06dc-4ce5-9e80-8fb0729eacd6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee null-termination of the destination string when the source string length is greater than or equal to the destination buffer size. This can lead to buffer overflows, which can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, overwrite data, or cause a system crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination of the destination string. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `std::string` class or the `strlcpy` function if available.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with `std::string::copy` function. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string socket_id = socket->get_socket_id();\nname = new char[socket_id.length() + 1];\nsocket_id.copy(name, socket_id.length());\nname[socket_id.length()] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` class.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that all links are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
