              "id": "glog-abd71a7f-a26f-419b-98d8-30a173b51c58",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to crashes, data corruption, and potentially code execution, making it a serious security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use safer string copy functions that include a length parameter. This allows the function to ensure that it does not write past the end of the buffer. Functions like `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy` can be used instead, but they must be used correctly to ensure safety.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strcpy`, you can use `strncpy` like this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar name[4];\nstrncpy(name, \"VCN\", sizeof(name));\nname[sizeof(name) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nIn this code, `strncpy` will copy at most `sizeof(name)` characters. If \"VCN\" is shorter than this, the remaining space in `name` will be filled with '\\0'. The last line ensures that `name` is null-terminated, which is necessary because `strncpy` does not null-terminate the string if the source is larger than the size parameter.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strncpy` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-2be077ce-4c60-42b3-b2c7-bf8d68ab0228",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to crashes, data corruption, and potentially code execution, making it a serious security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use safer string copy functions that include a length parameter. This allows the function to ensure that it does not write past the end of the buffer. Functions like `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy` can be used instead, but they must be used correctly to ensure safety.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar name[10];\nstrcpy(name, \"SDMA7\");\n```\n\nYou should use:\n\n```cpp\nchar name[10];\nstrncpy(name, \"SDMA7\", sizeof(name));\nname[sizeof(name) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nOr:\n\n```cpp\nchar name[10];\nstrlcpy(name, \"SDMA7\", sizeof(name));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcpy` function is part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\nThe `strlcpy` function is not standard, but is available on some systems. If available, it is declared in the `cstring` header as well.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-0f66fe39-c688-4d53-a628-2d8d46ffcc73",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to crashes, data corruption, and potentially code execution, making it a serious security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that include a length parameter. This allows the function to ensure that it does not write past the end of the buffer. Functions like `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy` can be used instead, but they must be used correctly to ensure safety.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer alternative. For example, you could use `strncpy` like this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar name[6];\nstrncpy(name, \"SDMA6\", sizeof(name));\nname[sizeof(name) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nThis code will copy the string \"SDMA6\" into the `name` buffer, but it will not write past the end of the buffer. The `sizeof(name)` function is used to get the size of the `name` buffer, and the `- 1` ensures that there is space for the null terminator.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library to provide the `strncpy` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
